(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["sbi-po"] = {"qs": [["Reasoning Ability", "Manoj walks 10 m north, turns right and walks 6 m, turns right again and walks 18 m, then turns left and walks 9 m. How far and in which direction is he from his starting point?", ["15 m, South-East", "17 m, South-East", "17 m, North-East", "13 m, South-West"], 1, "Net movement: east 6 + 9 = 15 m and south 18 − 10 = 8 m. Distance = √(15² + 8²) = 17 m. Being east and south of the start, he is to the South-East."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Five persons P, Q, R, S and T sit in a row facing north.\n• R sits exactly in the middle.\n• Q sits at one of the extreme ends.\n• S sits second to the left of R.\n• T does not sit next to Q.\n\nWho sits immediately to the left of Q?", ["P", "T", "S", "R"], 0, "R is 3rd, so S is 1st. Q must then be at the right end (5th). T cannot be next to Q, so T is 2nd and P is 4th, immediately to the left of Q."], ["English Language", "In the sentence 'A prudent lender checks the repayment capacity of every borrower', which word can replace PRUDENT without changing the meaning?", ["Reckless", "Generous", "Judicious", "Hasty"], 2, "'Prudent' means acting with care and good judgement, which matches 'judicious'. 'Reckless' and 'hasty' are near opposites, and 'generous' refers to willingness to give rather than careful judgement."], ["English Language", "Choose the connector that best fits the blank.\nThe pilot project earned steady profits for two years; ________, the board approved its expansion to fifty more branches.", ["nevertheless", "otherwise", "consequently", "meanwhile"], 2, "Profits leading to approval of expansion is a cause-and-effect link, so 'consequently' fits. 'Nevertheless' signals contrast, 'otherwise' signals an alternative condition and 'meanwhile' signals simultaneous events, none of which suit this logic."], ["English Language", "Select the pair of words that fits both blanks correctly.\nThe committee's report was ________ by the board, which promptly ________ most of its recommendations.", ["endorsed, adopted", "rejected, adopted", "ignored, implemented", "endorsed, discarded"], 0, "A board that approves a report would logically adopt its recommendations, so 'endorsed, adopted' is consistent. The other pairs contradict each other, for example rejecting a report and then adopting its recommendations."], ["English Language", "Fill in the blank with the most suitable word.\nDespite several reminders from the branch, the borrower remained ________ about repaying the overdue amount.", ["punctual", "indifferent", "diligent", "eager"], 1, "'Despite several reminders' signals that the borrower did not care, so 'indifferent' fits. 'Punctual', 'diligent' and 'eager' would all suggest willingness to repay, which contradicts the contrast set up by 'despite'."], ["Reasoning Ability", "A + B means A is the father of B; A − B means A is the wife of B; A × B means A is the brother of B; A ÷ B means A is the daughter of B.\nIf P ÷ Q − R × S, how is R related to P?", ["Uncle", "Brother", "Father", "Grandfather"], 2, "P is the daughter of Q, and Q is the wife of R, so R is P's father. R × S only tells us R is S's brother, making S P's uncle or aunt, not changing R's relation."], ["English Language", "Find the part of the sentence that has a grammatical error.\nHardly had the auditors (A) / entered the branch (B) / than the manager (C) / produced all the ledgers. (D)", ["(A)", "(B)", "(C)", "(D)"], 2, "'Hardly' and 'scarcely' are followed by 'when', not 'than'. The correct form is 'Hardly had the auditors entered the branch when the manager produced all the ledgers'. 'No sooner' is the phrase that takes 'than'."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Statements: All banks are buildings. Some buildings are offices. No office is a shop.\nConclusions: I. Some banks are offices. II. Some buildings are not shops.", ["Only conclusion I follows", "Both I and II follow", "Neither I nor II follows", "Only conclusion II follows"], 3, "The buildings that are offices cannot be shops, so some buildings are definitely not shops (II follows). Banks may or may not overlap with offices, so I is only a possibility and does not follow."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Pointing to a young man, Kavya said, 'His mother is the only daughter of my mother.' How is the young man related to Kavya?", ["Nephew", "Brother", "Cousin", "Son"], 3, "Kavya is a woman, so the only daughter of her mother is Kavya herself. The young man's mother is therefore Kavya, making him her son. 'Nephew' would require a sister, which the clue rules out."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "A train running at 72 km/h passes a signal pole in 15 seconds and crosses a platform in 25 seconds. What is the length of the platform?", ["200 m", "180 m", "250 m", "300 m"], 0, "72 km/h = 20 m/s. Train length = 20 × 15 = 300 m. Crossing the platform covers 20 × 25 = 500 m, so the platform is 500 − 300 = 200 m. The 300 m option is the train itself."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "Deposits (₹ crore) of three branches are given.\nBranch P: 2023 – 240, 2024 – 300\nBranch Q: 2023 – 180, 2024 – 210\nBranch R: 2023 – 280, 2024 – 340\nWhat is the approximate percentage increase in the combined deposits of the three branches from 2023 to 2024?", ["21.4%", "25.0%", "18.6%", "23.8%"], 0, "Combined deposits rise from 700 to 850 crore, an increase of 150. Percentage increase = 150/700 × 100 ≈ 21.4%. Branch P alone grew 25%, which is a distractor, not the combined figure."], ["English Language", "Choose the single word for: 'A form of government in which power rests with a small group of people'.", ["Monarchy", "Democracy", "Anarchy", "Oligarchy"], 3, "Oligarchy means rule by a few. Monarchy is rule by one sovereign, democracy is rule by the people through elected representatives, and anarchy is the absence of any government authority."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "Solve the two equations and find the relationship between x and y.\nI. x² − 11x + 30 = 0\nII. y² − 13y + 42 = 0", ["x > y", "x < y", "x ≤ y", "x ≥ y"], 2, "From I, x = 5 or 6. From II, y = 6 or 7. Every x is less than or equal to every y, and the case x = y = 6 rules out the strict relation x < y. Hence x ≤ y."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "A car travels from town X to town Y at 60 km/h and returns along the same road at 40 km/h. What is its average speed for the whole journey?", ["50 km/h", "48 km/h", "45 km/h", "52 km/h"], 1, "For equal distances, average speed = 2ab/(a + b) = 2 × 60 × 40/100 = 48 km/h. The simple average of 50 km/h is wrong because more time is spent at the slower speed."], ["English Language", "Pick the word that is the opposite of FRUGAL.", ["Economical", "Extravagant", "Thrifty", "Careful"], 1, "'Frugal' describes someone who spends sparingly. Its opposite is 'extravagant', meaning spending lavishly. 'Economical' and 'thrifty' are synonyms of frugal, and 'careful' is close in meaning, not opposite."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Six persons A, B, C, D, E and F live on the six floors of a building, one on each floor. The lowest floor is numbered 1 and the top floor 6.\n• C lives on an even-numbered floor above D.\n• Exactly two persons live between C and F.\n• A lives immediately above F.\n• D lives on an even-numbered floor, immediately above E.\n• B does not live on the top floor.\n\nWho lives on the floor immediately below B?", ["C", "F", "A", "D"], 2, "C must be on 6 (an even floor above an even floor D with E below it). Then F is on 3, A on 4, D on 2 and E on 1, leaving B on 5. A is directly below B."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "A bag has 4 red, 5 blue and 3 green balls. Two balls are drawn at random together. What is the probability that both are of the same colour?", ["13/66", "1/3", "19/66", "5/22"], 2, "Total ways = 12C2 = 66. Same colour: 4C2 + 5C2 + 3C2 = 6 + 10 + 3 = 19. Probability = 19/66. Leaving out the green pair gives 16/66, a common slip."], ["English Language", "Read the passage and answer the question that follows.\nMany first-time borrowers in small towns now take loans through mobile apps. The speed is welcome, but some apps hide charges in fine print and use aggressive recovery methods. Regulators have responded by requiring lenders to disclose the full annual cost of a loan upfront. Experts say disclosure helps, yet borrowers also need basic financial literacy to compare offers sensibly.\n\nWhich word is closest in meaning to 'aggressive' as used in the passage?", ["Generous", "Delayed", "Careless", "Forceful"], 3, "'Aggressive recovery methods' means pushy, forceful ways of collecting dues. 'Generous' is nearly opposite, while 'delayed' and 'careless' do not describe the pressure the passage is criticising."], ["Reasoning Ability", "In a row of students, Asha is 12th from the left and Bina is 18th from the right. When they exchange places, Asha becomes 20th from the left. How many students are in the row?", ["38", "36", "37", "30"], 2, "After exchanging, Asha occupies Bina's old seat, which is 20th from the left and 18th from the right. Total = 20 + 18 − 1 = 37. Asha's original position is not needed."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "In how many ways can the letters of the word BANKER be arranged so that the vowels always come together?", ["120", "240", "720", "360"], 1, "The vowels A and E form one unit, giving 5 units that can be arranged in 5! = 120 ways. The vowels can swap among themselves in 2 ways, so the total is 120 × 2 = 240."], ["English Language", "Read the passage and answer the question that follows.\nMany first-time borrowers in small towns now take loans through mobile apps. The speed is welcome, but some apps hide charges in fine print and use aggressive recovery methods. Regulators have responded by requiring lenders to disclose the full annual cost of a loan upfront. Experts say disclosure helps, yet borrowers also need basic financial literacy to compare offers sensibly.\n\nWhat is the central concern raised in the passage?", ["Fast digital loans can harm borrowers who do not understand their true cost", "Mobile apps have made banks in small towns completely unnecessary", "Regulators have banned digital lenders from operating in small towns", "Borrowers in small towns prefer slow loans from moneylenders"], 0, "The passage welcomes speed but warns of hidden charges and harsh recovery, then stresses disclosure and literacy. Nothing says banks are unnecessary or that lending is banned, so only the first statement captures its central concern."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Six persons A, B, C, D, E and F live on the six floors of a building, one on each floor. The lowest floor is numbered 1 and the top floor 6.\n• C lives on an even-numbered floor above D.\n• Exactly two persons live between C and F.\n• A lives immediately above F.\n• D lives on an even-numbered floor, immediately above E.\n• B does not live on the top floor.\n\nHow many persons live between E and A?", ["One", "Two", "Three", "None"], 1, "The arrangement from bottom is E(1), D(2), F(3), A(4), B(5), C(6). Between E on floor 1 and A on floor 4 are D and F, so two persons live between them."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "One number in the series below does not follow the pattern. Identify it.\n2, 5, 11, 23, 47, 96, 191", ["47", "23", "191", "96"], 3, "Each term is twice the previous term plus 1: 2→5→11→23→47→95→191. So 96 is wrong and should be 95. Note that 191 = 2 × 95 + 1 also confirms the pattern."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Statements: Some pens are pencils. No pencil is an eraser.\nConclusions: I. Some pens are not erasers. II. All erasers being pens is a possibility.", ["Only conclusion I follows", "Only conclusion II follows", "Both I and II follow", "Neither I nor II follows"], 2, "Pens that are pencils can never be erasers, so I follows. Erasers can lie wholly inside the pen circle as long as they avoid the pencil region, so II is a valid possibility. Both follow."], ["Reasoning Ability", "In a certain code, PLANT is written as QNDRY. How is SAVER written in that code?", ["TCYIW", "TBYIV", "TCXIW", "UCYIW"], 0, "The letters are shifted forward by 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 places in order: P+1=Q, L+2=N, A+3=D, N+4=R, T+5=Y. Applying the same to SAVER gives T, C, Y, I, W."], ["English Language", "Find the part of the sentence that has a grammatical error.\nEach of the applicants (A) / have been asked (B) / to submit (C) / the documents by Friday. (D)", ["(A)", "(B)", "(C)", "(D)"], 1, "The subject is 'Each', which is singular, so the verb must be 'has been asked', not 'have been asked'. The phrase 'of the applicants' does not change the number of the subject."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "A can finish a task in 20 days and B in 30 days. They work together for 6 days, after which A leaves. In how many more days will B finish the remaining task?", ["12 days", "18 days", "15 days", "10 days"], 2, "Together they do 1/20 + 1/30 = 1/12 per day, so in 6 days they finish half the task. B alone needs 30 × 1/2 = 15 days for the remaining half."], ["English Language", "What does the idiom 'a red herring' mean in the sentence: 'The rumour about a merger was a red herring during the audit'?", ["Something that distracts attention from the real issue", "A financial loss that is hidden in the accounts", "A warning sign that predicts an approaching crisis", "A rare opportunity that must be grabbed at once"], 0, "A red herring is a misleading clue that draws attention away from the actual matter. It does not refer to a hidden loss, a warning sign or an opportunity, though these may sound related in a banking context."], ["English Language", "Arrange the sentences P, Q, R and S in a logical order.\nP: As a result, the branch decided to open on Saturdays as well.\nQ: Footfall at the new branch was far higher than expected.\nR: The extra day reduced waiting time considerably.\nS: Long queues began forming on weekdays.", ["SQPR", "QPSR", "QSRP", "QSPR"], 3, "Q introduces high footfall, S states its effect (long queues), P gives the response ('As a result', Saturday opening) and R reports the outcome of that extra day. Other orders place the result before its cause."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "Find the value of ?\n35% of 840 + 2/3 of 432 = ?", ["572", "582", "594", "588"], 1, "35% of 840 = 294 and two-thirds of 432 = 288. Adding them gives 294 + 288 = 582. Computing percentages and fractions separately before adding avoids most calculation slips in simplification questions."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Study the sequence: M 3 # K 8 R @ 5 T % 2 Q 9 & L\nHow many symbols are immediately preceded by a number and immediately followed by a letter?", ["One", "Two", "Three", "Four"], 1, "Check each symbol: '#' has 3 before and K after (counts); '@' has R before; '%' has T before; '&' has 9 before and L after (counts). So exactly two symbols meet the condition."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Statements: P ≥ Q > R = S; T < S ≤ U\nConclusions: I. P > T  II. U ≥ R", ["Only conclusion I follows", "Both I and II follow", "Only conclusion II follows", "Neither I nor II follows"], 1, "Combining, P ≥ Q > R = S > T gives P > T, so I is true. Also U ≥ S = R gives U ≥ R, so II is true. Both conclusions follow."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "A starts a business with ₹24,000. Five months later B joins with ₹36,000. At the end of the year the profit is ₹30,000. What is B's share?", ["₹16,000", "₹15,000", "₹18,000", "₹14,000"], 3, "Capital-time: A = 24,000 × 12 = 2,88,000; B = 36,000 × 7 = 2,52,000. Ratio 8 : 7. B's share = 30,000 × 7/15 = ₹14,000. Forgetting that B invested for only 7 months gives wrong options."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "Find the next term of the series: 3, 7, 16, 32, 57, ?", ["93", "89", "91", "97"], 0, "The differences are 4, 9, 16 and 25, which are squares of 2, 3, 4 and 5. The next difference is 6² = 36, so the next term is 57 + 36 = 93."], ["English Language", "Read the passage and answer the question that follows.\nMany first-time borrowers in small towns now take loans through mobile apps. The speed is welcome, but some apps hide charges in fine print and use aggressive recovery methods. Regulators have responded by requiring lenders to disclose the full annual cost of a loan upfront. Experts say disclosure helps, yet borrowers also need basic financial literacy to compare offers sensibly.\n\nAccording to the passage, why is disclosure alone considered insufficient?", ["Lenders can easily ignore the disclosure requirement", "The annual cost of a loan cannot be calculated in advance", "Mobile apps do not allow lenders to display any charges", "Borrowers must also be able to compare loan offers sensibly"], 3, "The last sentence says disclosure helps, yet borrowers also need financial literacy to compare offers. The passage does not claim lenders ignore the rule or that costs cannot be calculated."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "The length of a rectangular hall is increased by 20% and its breadth is decreased by 20%. What is the effect on its area?", ["No change", "Increases by 4%", "Decreases by 2%", "Decreases by 4%"], 3, "New area = 1.2 × 0.8 = 0.96 of the original, a 4% decrease. Equal percentage increase and decrease never cancel out; the net effect is always a fall of x²/100 per cent."], ["English Language", "Improve the part in capitals, if needed.\nIf I WOULD HAVE KNOWN about the deadline, I would have applied much earlier.", ["had known", "would know", "have known", "No improvement required"], 0, "In a third conditional the if-clause takes the past perfect: 'If I had known…, I would have applied'. 'Would have' should appear only in the main clause, so the sentence needs correction."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "A 40-litre mixture contains milk and water in the ratio 5 : 3. How much water must be added to make the ratio 1 : 1?", ["8 litres", "12 litres", "15 litres", "10 litres"], 3, "Milk = 25 litres and water = 15 litres. For a 1 : 1 ratio, water must equal milk, i.e. 25 litres, so 25 − 15 = 10 litres of water must be added."], ["English Language", "Find the part of the sentence that has a grammatical error.\nThe volume of digital payments (A) / in rural districts (B) / have risen steadily (C) / over the last five years. (D)", ["(A)", "(B)", "(C)", "(D)"], 2, "The head noun of the subject is 'volume', which is singular, so the verb should be 'has risen'. The plural 'payments' and 'districts' are inside prepositional phrases and do not decide the verb."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "The difference between the compound interest (compounded annually) and the simple interest on a sum for 2 years at 8% per annum is ₹96. What is the sum?", ["₹15,000", "₹12,000", "₹16,000", "₹18,000"], 0, "For 2 years, CI − SI = P × (r/100)². So 96 = P × 0.0064, giving P = ₹15,000. This shortcut works only for a two-year period with annual compounding."], ["Reasoning Ability", "In a secret language, 'loan is approved' is coded 'ka zo ri', 'approved by manager' is coded 'zo lu pe' and 'manager is busy' is coded 'pe ka mi'. Which code stands for 'by'?", ["zo", "pe", "ri", "lu"], 3, "'approved' = zo (common to first two), 'manager' = pe (common to last two) and 'is' = ka. In 'approved by manager', the remaining code lu must mean 'by'. 'ri' stands for 'loan'."], ["Reasoning Ability", "If the letters of the word BRANDS are arranged in alphabetical order, how many letters will remain in the same position?", ["Two", "One", "Three", "None"], 0, "Alphabetical order gives A B D N R S. Comparing with B R A N D S position by position, only N (4th) and S (6th) stay unchanged, so two letters remain in place."], ["Reasoning Ability", "Statements: A > B ≥ C; D ≤ C < E; F = E\nConclusions: I. F > D  II. B > D", ["Only conclusion I follows", "Only conclusion II follows", "Both I and II follow", "Neither I nor II follows"], 0, "F = E > C ≥ D gives F > D, so I follows. For II, B ≥ C ≥ D only gives B ≥ D; B could equal D, so B > D is not certain. Only I follows."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "A shopkeeper marks an article 40% above its cost price and then allows a discount of 15% on the marked price. What is his profit percentage?", ["25%", "19%", "21%", "17%"], 1, "Take cost price as 100. Marked price = 140 and selling price = 140 × 0.85 = 119. Profit = 19 on 100, i.e. 19%. Simply subtracting 15 from 40 to get 25% is a common error."]]};
